{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Deployment Diagram"}, {"type": "position", "content": 9}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear/use when you talk about deployment diagrams:"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Give detailed information related to UML and common related topics."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations.  Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob09re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "require "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to demand, to ask for or to need something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I'm checking the guidelines and that's what the clients require."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob09aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "middleware "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "intermediate that assists in communications between devices or systems"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: In this project, we will need to verify how software, middleware and hardware connect and interact with each other."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob09aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "embedded "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "fixed, rooted or encased"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We might use it to explore the way embedded systems and their nodes interact by showing the type of connection that they establish."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob09aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "careful "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "attentive, thorough or mindful "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: If we decide to use this diagram we should be careful with notation. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob09aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "large "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "very big, enormous or extended"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: These diagrams might become too large, so the most logical thing to do is to agree on a fixed notation to prevent confusions.  "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob09aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "maintain "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "support, help or assist"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We may need to learn more about the system, and I think we will have to install it, maintain it and support it."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob09aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I get more information on deployment diagrams. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I select and organize relevant information on the topic.   "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share my findings with the class."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for more information about deployment diagrams."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Select and organize those features you consider interesting. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Make a presentation to share your finding with the class. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Superlatives"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use <strong>superlatives</strong> to talk about a <strong>noun</strong> or <strong>group of nouns</strong> that have a highly-developed <strong>quality</strong>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "In order to use <strong>superlatives</strong> with short adjectives, you need to use the definite article <strong>the</strong>, and the adjective with the <strong>-est </strong>suffix. </br></br>  Remember, short adjectives have only one or two syllables. </br></br>  Examples: </br> <span class=\"italic\"><strong>The fastest</strong> way to determine the right project is by analyzing the client's need. </br> Digital tools offer <strong>the simplest</strong> approach to organize modeling diagrams.</span> </br></br>  When you have <strong>long adjectives</strong> (two or more syllables), you need to use the definite article <strong>the</strong>, the particle <strong>most </strong>and the adjective. </br></br>  Examples: </br>  <span class=\"italic\"><strong>The most challenging </strong>part of doing a diagram is to follow it. </br> Even <strong>the most advanced</strong> program can't create a bug-free system.</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Well, for this project I think the most appropriate diagram is Deployment. I'm  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Sure this model will be the most helpful."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Modal Verbs "}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use the <strong>modal verbs</strong> <strong>may, might, will</strong> and <strong>would</strong> to talk about possibilities in the future."}, {"type": "text", "content": "These <strong>modal verbs </strong>vary in degree when talking about possibilities, will expresses a high probability of something happening, while might expresses a low probability. </br></br>  Will \u2013 <strong>strong probability</strong></br> May</br> Would</br> Might- <strong>weak possibility</strong></br></br>  In order to use them you need to follow this structure </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> subject + modal verb + main verb (base form) + complement</div></br>  Examples: </br></br>  <span class=\"italic\">We <strong>may </strong>switch to a better diagram soon. </br> There <strong>might </strong>be more candidates if we offer more benefits. </span></br></br>  In negative sentences, you put Not after the modal verb and then the main verb. </br></br>  Examples: </br></br>  <span class=\"italic\">Our diagrams <strong>will not</strong> be very accurate with little data. </br> There <strong>would not </strong>be too many software architects who use that notation system. </span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "In this project, we will need to verify how software, middleware and hardware connect and interact with each other. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "We might want to try others, don't you think? "}, {"type": "example", "content": "They may contain artifacts, software components, a deployment specification and even other nodes."}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1}, {"_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob09re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<strong>Charles</strong>: Well, for this project I think the most appropriate diagram is Deployment. I'm checking the guidelines and that's what the clients require. </br>\n<strong>John</strong>: Really? Why? </br>\n<strong>Charles</strong>: Because deployment diagrams present a fixed view of the processing nodes. In this project, we will need to verify how software, middleware, and hardware connect and interact with each other. </br>\n<strong>John</strong>: We need to know about that. I'm reading here that this project needs those static views. But, are you sure that this is the most adequate model? We might want to try others, don't you think? </br>"}, {"time": "38500", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob09re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<strong>Charles</strong>: I'm sure this model will be the most helpful. We may be able to check how different machines work. For example, we would monitor the interactions between servers, laptops and mobile devices. That may be very convenient. </br>\n<strong>John</strong>: I see. Are there any other advantages to deployment diagrams? </br>\n<strong>Charles</strong>: I think they're one of the most versatile models for software artifacts and architecture. We might use it to explore the way embedded systems and their nodes interact by showing the type of connection that they establish. </br>\n<strong>John</strong>: When you talk about nodes, what do they contain? </br>"}, {"time": "77000", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob09re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<strong>Charles</strong>: They may contain artifacts, software components, a deployment specification and even other nodes. But, if we decide to use this diagram we should be careful with notation. </br>\n<strong>John</strong>: I was going to mention that. These diagrams might become too large, so the most logical thing to do is to agree on a fixed notation to prevent confusions. But I agree with you, especially because we may need to learn more about the system, and I think we will have to install it, maintain it, and support it. </br>\n<strong>Charles</strong>: Exactly! As I said, this is the most convenient kind of diagram for this project. Let's start modeling, then!"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n4le02ob09re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n4le02ob09re2aud01"}, "type": "slid